ull,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jon.4.1-7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.1","r":"Jon.4.1","rawR":"Jon.4.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.1","value":"Jon.4.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["This change of plans"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["It"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): See ",{"href":"?item=Jon.3.10_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Jon.3.10_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Jon.3.10_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 3:10"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Jon.4.1","refs":[{"book":"JON","endChapter":4,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Jon.4.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JON","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"fd9724ca874da99aed4d78a16a3860f77c944755a8b626fcb7f61dd76709fc90","sourceItemName":"Jon.4.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11239,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JON","canonicalOSIS":"Jonah","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Jon"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Jon.4.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jon.4.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.2","r":"Jon.4.2","rawR":"Jon.4.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.2","value":"Jon.4.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Jonah expresses his motive in originally fleeing from the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Jon.1.3","r":"Jon.1.3","rawR":"Jon.1.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.1.3","value":"Jon.1.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:3"},"): He knew that the Lord would not destroy the wicked Ninevites if they repented. • ",{"c":["I knew:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Jonah practically quotes ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.34.6-7","r":"Exod.34.6-7","rawR":"Exod.34.6-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.34.6-7","value":"Exod.34.6-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 34:6-7"},", a passage set in the context of Israel’s covenant relationship with the Lord. Even in the Old Testament, God was concerned to spread salvation to the nations (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.28.18-20","r":"Matt.28.18-20","rawR":"Matt.28.18-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.28.18-20","value":"Matt.28.18-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 28:18-20"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Jon.4.2","refs":[{"book":"JON","endChapter":4,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Jon.4.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JON","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"d8b54b3a74d067fa8fdecdc02823054b937899a5695ebe4531ce92c935de354a","sourceItemName":"Jon.4.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11240,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JON","canonicalOSIS":"Jonah","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Jon"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Jon.4.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jon.4.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.3-5","r":"Jon.4.3-5","rawR":"Jon.4.3-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.3-5","value":"Jon.4.3-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:3-5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Jonah’s desire to die rather than embrace God’s will (see also ",{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.8-9","r":"Jon.4.8-9","rawR":"Jon.4.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.8-9","value":"Jon.4.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:8-9"},"), and his willingness to wait in hope that the city would be destroyed (",{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.5","r":"Jon.4.5","rawR":"Jon.4.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.5","value":"Jon.4.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:5"},"), are signs of his hard-heartedness and his hatred for the Assyrians."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Jon.4.3-5","refs":[{"book":"JON","endChapter":4,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Jon.4.3-5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JON","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"489fb973d29ccf5aef52bbcdda07c4e01eec47b2df6d6c3c505a2ca829288e45","sourceItemName":"Jon.4.3-5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11241,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JON","canonicalOSIS":"Jonah","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Jon"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Jon.4.3-5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jon.4.3-5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.4","r":"Jon.4.4","rawR":"Jon.4.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.4","value":"Jon.4.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The Lord’s reply is a rhetorical question. The implied answer is “No, of course not!” The object lesson that follows (",{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.6-11","r":"Jon.4.6-11","rawR":"Jon.4.6-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.6-11","value":"Jon.4.6-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6-11"},") reveals why Jonah ought not to be angry."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Jon.4.4","refs":[{"book":"JON","endChapter":4,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Jon.4.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JON","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"fd166c9f7c4123b70dd868649436be340e41ea4d48d88dfc56908adc7c6ce938","sourceItemName":"Jon.4.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11242,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JON","canonicalOSIS":"Jonah","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Jon"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Jon.4.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jon.4.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.6","r":"Jon.4.6","rawR":"Jon.4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.6","value":"Jon.4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["The"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"}," is God’s covenant name in relation to Israel (see, e.g., ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.72.18","r":"Ps.72.18","rawR":"Ps.72.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.72.18","value":"Ps.72.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 72:18"},"). ",{"c":["God"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," did these things to show his covenant love toward Jonah and to those in Israel who had the same attitude. • ",{"c":["arranged for:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," see ",{"href":"?item=Jon.1.17_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Jon.1.17_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Jon.1.17_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on Jon 1:17"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Jon.4.6","refs":[{"book":"JON","endChapter":4,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Jon.4.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JON","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ecff91782e5d090f1289d70a28bdd49eff8c1ff277c80e074b47e9cb6a1cf7d5","sourceItemName":"Jon.4.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11243,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JON","canonicalOSIS":"Jonah","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Jon"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Jon.4.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jon.4.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Jon.4.11","r":"Jon.4.11","rawR":"Jon.4.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jon.4.11","value":"Jon.4.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["people living in spiritual darkness:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," God graciously sent the light of his prophetic word into a wicked city. Not all who encounter God’s light respond favorably (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=John.3.19-21","r":"John.3.19-21","rawR":"John.3.19-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.3.19-21","value":"John.3.19-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"John 3:19-21"},"), but God is eager to save those who receive his word in genuine repentance and faith. • The book closes abruptly, leaving the reader to ponder God’s final question to Jonah. God would rather save than destroy. Those who have received his mercy must be glad for that same mercy to be shown to others, even to their enemies."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Jon.4.11","refs":[{"book":"JON","endChapter":4,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Jon.4.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"JON","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"da64230d4b547927291e4e5334044d31076f6736fe6c85d8bc07c26454dc4563","sourceItemName":"Jon.4.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11244,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"JON","canonicalOSIS":"Jonah","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Jon"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Jon.4.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jon.4.11","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}